# --- endpoint credentials ---------------------------------------------------
# One variable per endpoint your models.yaml defines, named by its `key_env`.
#
# An endpoint whose variable is unset is dropped with a warning and its models
# go with it, so one catalogue works across a fleet holding different subsets of
# the keys. Startup still refuses a deployment whose *default* model is gone.

MINIMAX_API_KEY=${YOUR_API_KEY}
# OPENAI_API_KEY=${YOUR_API_KEY}

# --- kingfisher -------------------------------------------------------------

# Where kingfisher sends prompts and what it runs there is not set here: it is
# models.yaml, and models.yaml.example is the annotated copy to start from.
#
# Defaults to <workspace>/models.yaml. Point several deployments at one file and
# they share a reviewed catalogue; the example follows it there.
# KINGFISHER_MODELS_FILE=/absolute/path/to/catalogue/models.yaml

# Wire formats of your own, for an endpoint's `api` to name beside the two
# kingfisher ships. `module:name` of something callable with no arguments that
# returns a mapping of name to `kingfisher.Adapter`. Python rather than a block
# in models.yaml because a row names a class to import, and that file is meant
# to be shared and reviewed as data. See docs/guides/configuration.md.
# KINGFISHER_ADAPTERS_FACTORY=mycompany.models:adapters

# The vocabulary of source ids this deployment declares, and which contain
# which. Who reaches what is written in the definitions themselves -- this file
# is only the names those lines are checked against, so a mistyped source id is
# an error rather than a source id nobody holds. Defaults to
# <workspace>/source_ids.yaml and relocates so a fleet shares one set of names.
#
# Writing one turns source-id access on: every call must then say who is making
# it (--as A,B on the command, source_ids=[...] in the library,
# source_ids=UNSCOPED for a call with no caller), and a definition carrying no
# `source_ids:` line is reachable by everyone -- startup names each one. A file
# that is present and will not parse stops the deployment. See docs/formats.md.
# KINGFISHER_SOURCE_IDS_FILE=/absolute/path/to/source_ids.yaml

# One process serves one workspace. Keep it outside this repository so runtime
# state does not land in kingfisher's own tree.
#
# Use an ABSOLUTE path: `~` resolves against HOME, which is not stable in every
# execution context, and a workspace that silently relocates takes its sessions,
# memory and skills with it.
#
# Nothing here is versioned. What is worth keeping is the authored tier, and
# each of those relocates on its own below: point them at a directory you
# version and the sessions stay disposable.
KINGFISHER_WORKSPACE=/absolute/path/to/kingfisher-workspace

# Where `kingfisher seed` copies definitions FROM -- the opposite direction to
# the catalogue directories below, which say where a definition is read.
#
# Relative, and it resolves against your WORKING DIRECTORY, so it is found from
# the root of a checkout and not from a subdirectory -- the same rule `.env`
# itself follows. `kingfisher seed --from DIR` overrides it for one run.
#
# It points at `./assets_examples` because that is this repository's own worked
# set, committed and there to be read -- so a fresh checkout seeds without
# editing anything. Definitions you fetched from somewhere else go in
# `./assets`, which is git-ignored for the reason its README gives, and pointing
# this there is how a deployment runs its own. The variable names a role, not
# either of the two directories that can play it.
KINGFISHER_ASSETS=./assets_examples

# The session backends the `kingfisher` command runs on, when they are not
# kingfisher's own: `module:name` for something callable with no arguments that
# returns a SessionBackends. Only the command reads it -- a deployment building
# Kingfisher in code passes `backends=` -- and it is what lets `kingfisher
# sessions`, `reap` and `artifact` see sessions yours keep somewhere else.
#
# Unset, sessions are directories under <workspace>/sessions, which is right
# wherever this host may hold data. Where it may not -- a memory filesystem --
# mount durable storage there, or name session backends that keep them;
# `kingfisher doctor` refuses that arrangement with neither.
#
# KINGFISHER_SESSION_STORE and KINGFISHER_SESSION_STORE_FACTORY were removed
# with the store they configured, and are refused if still set, as is
# KINGFISHER_BACKEND_FACTORY, this setting's old name.
# KINGFISHER_SESSION_BACKENDS_FACTORY=mycompany.sessions:build_backends

# Nothing signs what kingfisher keeps about a session -- the agent it is pinned
# to, the conversation, a paused turn. What keeps the agent's shell from
# rewriting them is the shell's fence: the sandbox below, or a backend of yours
# that passes `shell_denied` in BACKEND_CONTRACT. `kingfisher doctor` warns
# where it can see neither.

# There is nothing here for harness state, and there used to be two settings.
# STATE_DIR and SCRATCH_DIR moved run logs, session claims, pinned agents and the
# shell's TMPDIR out of the workspace; all four are inside their sessions now, so
# they are swept when a session is and counted against its quota. What is left in
# <workspace>/.kingfisher is the marker and the sandbox profile, which describe
# the workspace rather than any session in it and have nowhere else to be.

# Where the definition catalogues live -- one per kind. All default inside the
# workspace and relocate independently: share the procedures while keeping your
# own delegates, or the reverse. Unlike the session directories these hold
# authored content, so several deployments pointing at one reviewed copy is the
# point.
#
# TOOLS_DIR and MIDDLEWARES_DIR are the two worth a second thought: both are
# *code*, imported into this process and deliberately not backend routes, so no
# file tool reaches either.
# KINGFISHER_AGENTS_DIR=/absolute/path/to/catalogue/agents
# KINGFISHER_MIDDLEWARES_DIR=/absolute/path/to/catalogue/middlewares
# KINGFISHER_SKILLS_DIR=/absolute/path/to/catalogue/skills
# KINGFISHER_SUBAGENTS_DIR=/absolute/path/to/catalogue/subagents
# KINGFISHER_TOOLS_DIR=/absolute/path/to/catalogue/tools

# Further skills directories, each read as one more folder of the catalogue:
# `vendor=/opt/vendor/skills` is offered at /skills/vendor/ and granted as
# `vendor::<name>`. For a party that cannot write into the catalogue itself.
# Skills sit directly in each one, and nothing creates or seeds it. The shell
# reaches a mount's scripts as $KINGFISHER_SKILLS/vendor/..., like any folder's.
# KINGFISHER_SKILLS_MOUNTS=vendor=/opt/vendor/skills:team=/srv/team/skills

# Extra directories on the agent shell's PATH, colon-separated.
#
# The shell runs with an explicit allowlist rather than the parent environment,
# so it resolves only what is on it: the project's own bin, then
# /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin.
# Anything installed elsewhere is invisible until named here, and the failure
# looks like the tool not existing rather than like a PATH problem. Homebrew is
# the usual case on macOS: pdftotext, ripgrep and jq live in /opt/homebrew/bin.
# KINGFISHER_SHELL_PATH_EXTRA=/opt/homebrew/bin

# Which model a delegate runs is not set here either. It is one optional line in
# the subagent's own .yaml, naming an entry in models.yaml:
#
#     model: gpt-5
#
# Omit it and the delegate runs the default.

# --- limits ------------------------------------------------------------------

# How long one shell command or one interpreter run may take. Not the model
# timeout, which is per-model in models.yaml.
# KINGFISHER_EXECUTION_TIMEOUT_S=120
#
# How many graph steps one turn may take. Each agent turn costs two or three.
# KINGFISHER_RECURSION_LIMIT=150
#
# How many threads `astream` and `arun` hand blocking work to: the short work a
# turn does on this host, and any session-backends method left to its default,
# which runs the sync one here. One pool per process, so a second service asking
# for a different number is refused when it is built. Raise it for many turns at
# once on remote session backends whose async methods are those defaults.
# KINGFISHER_THREAD_POOL_SIZE=16

# What one session may consume. These protect the process from one runaway
# session; kingfisher cannot bound a *caller*, because it never knows who is.
#
# A turn's wall clock. RECURSION_LIMIT bounds graph steps and a model call has
# its own timeout; nothing bounds their product. On breach the turn stops
# between steps and returns what it produced, marked -- its files are on disk
# and in the result's manifest either way.
# KINGFISHER_TURN_TIMEOUT_S=3600
#
# How much disk one session may hold. Unset means unbounded, and it is checked
# before a turn starts rather than during one.
# KINGFISHER_SESSION_MAX_BYTES=1073741824
#
# How long an idle session survives a sweep: the age `kingfisher reap` uses when
# you do not name one, and what `reap()` falls back to when a janitor calls it
# without saying. Nothing sweeps on a schedule, so one of those has to run --
# `kingfisher sessions` shows what is waiting for it.
# KINGFISHER_SESSION_TTL_S=604800

# Whether a turn remembers the one before it. On by default.
#
# Off makes a deployment stateless in the only sense kingfisher can be: no
# checkpointer, so no database to contend on, orphan or vacuum. Files are
# unaffected -- /inputs, /outputs and /memory are on disk and a resumed session
# still finds them; it is the conversation that goes. Worth turning off for a
# request/response API, where a caller sends one task and never follows up.
# KINGFISHER_CONVERSATION_ENABLED=false

# Capabilities. Each flag gates the middleware AND its prompt section, so the
# agent is never told about something it cannot actually do. Both cost tokens on
# every turn rather than only when used -- the skills preamble is ~450 before a
# single skill is named -- which is why they are switches and not inferred from
# whether a catalogue happens to hold anything.
#
# `_ENABLED` because the bare `KINGFISHER_SKILLS` is already taken: it is the
# variable the agent's shell gets, holding the *path* to the catalogue, so a
# skill's scripts can find their neighbours. Setting the old name to that path
# is the mistake this suffix exists to stop.
# KINGFISHER_SKILLS_ENABLED=true
# KINGFISHER_MEMORY_ENABLED=true

# A JavaScript sandbox the agent can compute in: capped memory and time, no
# filesystem, no network, and reachable tools limited to what the request
# granted -- the one execution surface `execute` can never be, which is why a
# deployment may want both. Subagents can be dispatched from inside it, in a
# loop over a list the code computed; `spikes/dynamic_subagents.py` is that.
# KINGFISHER_INTERPRETER_ENABLED=true

# Who keeps `execute` off the rest of the host. The shell is a real shell: it
# runs with your permissions and reaches any file you can, whatever the file
# tools are rooted at. Measured unconfined, it could read this deployment's own
# API keys out of .env, plus ~/.aws and ~/.config/gh -- and `http_fetch` is one
# tool call away from sending them somewhere.
#
#   auto        (default) use whatever the platform offers. macOS: sandbox-exec,
#               denying your home directory while keeping the workspace, the
#               venv and the interpreter readable, and denying writes anywhere
#               but the workspace, $TMPDIR and /dev. Linux: Landlock where the
#               kernel and `kingfisher[fence]` allow it, else bubblewrap, else
#               a warning. Elsewhere: nothing, and it says so.
#   bubblewrap  bubblewrap even where Landlock would run, which also closes the
#               shell's network. Needs a seccomp profile permitting user
#               namespaces, which Docker's default denies; warns if it cannot.
#   external    a container already mounts only the workspace, so nothing is
#               wrapped and nothing is warned about.
#   off         deliberately unconfined. Warned about on every start.
# KINGFISHER_SHELL_SANDBOX=auto

# --- retired: read by nothing, and `kingfisher doctor` reports one -----------
# Nothing warns about an unknown KINGFISHER_ variable, so a line surviving an
# upgrade used to stop working in silence -- the deployment ran on the default
# and nothing connected the behaviour to the line that used to cause it.
# `doctor` is what replaced reading both names, and it is the only thing that
# will ever mention one of these, so run it after an upgrade.
#
# Listed under an arrow rather than as assignments, so this file does not
# re-advertise a spelling it is telling you to stop using.
#
#   KINGFISHER_SKILLS             -> KINGFISHER_SKILLS_ENABLED
#   KINGFISHER_MEMORY             -> KINGFISHER_MEMORY_ENABLED
#   KINGFISHER_INTERPRETER        -> KINGFISHER_INTERPRETER_ENABLED
#   KINGFISHER_CONVERSATION       -> KINGFISHER_CONVERSATION_ENABLED
#   KINGFISHER_API_STYLE          -> models.yaml, where an endpoint names the
#                                    wire format it speaks
#   KINGFISHER_MODEL              -> models.yaml, where a model names an endpoint
#   KINGFISHER_MAX_TOKENS         -> models.yaml, with the model's params beside it
#   KINGFISHER_TIMEOUT_S          -> KINGFISHER_EXECUTION_TIMEOUT_S above, and
#                                    timeout_s per model in models.yaml
#   KINGFISHER_MODEL_SUBAGENT     -> `model:` in the subagent's own .yaml
#   KINGFISHER_PROVIDER_SUBAGENT  -> the endpoint that model names
#   KINGFISHER_STATE_DIR          -> nothing: run logs, claims and pinned agents
#                                    sit inside the session they belong to
#   KINGFISHER_SCRATCH_DIR        -> nothing: TMPDIR is a directory inside the
#                                    session, swept and counted with it
#   KINGFISHER_SESSION_KEY        -> nothing: what a session keeps is no longer
#                                    signed; `doctor`'s session files check says
#                                    what keeps the shell out of it
#
# KINGFISHER_SERVER_* and KINGFISHER_SERVICE_* configured the HTTP service, which
# is no longer part of kingfisher. `doctor` reports any still set.
